            {
              "id": "glog-f9843eff-e7aa-42d8-bd40-9a65e3f54d1f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe YAML Load Vulnerability in Python refers to a security flaw that occurs when a program uses the `yaml.load()` function from the PyYAML library to deserialize untrusted data. This function can execute arbitrary Python commands, making it a serious security risk if the input is not controlled or sanitized. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability to execute arbitrary code, leading to potential system compromise.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use the `safe_load()` function instead of `load()`. The `safe_load()` function can only parse a subset of YAML and does not allow arbitrary code execution. Always avoid deserializing data from untrusted sources.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace:\n\n```python\ndata = yaml.load(inf)\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```python\ndata = yaml.safe_load(inf)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the PyYAML library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B506"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-927103af-c3a3-4490-a316-3224f804fd74",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe YAML Load Vulnerability in Python refers to a security flaw that occurs when a program uses the `yaml.load()` function from the PyYAML library to deserialize untrusted data. This function can execute arbitrary Python commands, making it a serious security risk if the input is not controlled or sanitized. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability to execute arbitrary code, leading to potential system compromise.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use the `safe_load()` function instead of `load()`. The `safe_load()` function can only parse a subset of YAML and does not allow arbitrary code execution. Always avoid deserializing data from untrusted sources.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace:\n\n```python\ndata = yaml.load(inf)\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```python\ndata = yaml.safe_load(inf)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the PyYAML library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B506"
                ]
