            {
              "id": "glog-106a73a8-8334-46be-8133-4508623b2e21",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `Unsafe YAML Load Vulnerability` in Python is a security vulnerability that occurs when the `load()` function of the PyYAML library is used. This function can execute arbitrary Python commands resulting from the deserialization of malicious YAML input. This can lead to a variety of attacks, including remote code execution, if the YAML input is from an untrusted source.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use the `safe_load()` function instead of `load()`. The `safe_load()` function can only deserialize a subset of YAML and it does not allow arbitrary code execution.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace:\n\n```python\nimport yaml\ndata = yaml.load(inFile)\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```python\nimport yaml\ndata = yaml.safe_load(inFile)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe library dependency required by the code example to execute properly is PyYAML.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B506"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-ee434889-c4d9-45a4-9ebb-34e812ecd1d8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `Unsafe YAML Load Vulnerability` in Python is a security vulnerability that occurs when a program uses the `yaml.load()` function from the PyYAML library to parse untrusted input. This function can execute arbitrary Python commands embedded in the YAML input, leading to a potential Remote Code Execution (RCE) vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `yaml.load()` with untrusted input. Instead, use the `yaml.safe_load()` function, which does not execute arbitrary Python commands.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace:\n\n```python\nimport yaml\ndata = yaml.load(f)\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```python\nimport yaml\ndata = yaml.safe_load(f)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the PyYAML library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B506"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-3a892611-24f6-44b0-bb27-6b2d2a6323aa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn Python, the `eval()` function is used to evaluate the Python expression which is passed to it as a string. However, it is considered dangerous as it can execute potentially harmful, arbitrary code. This is especially true if you are using `eval()` to evaluate user-supplied strings, which can lead to a high risk of code injection attacks.\n\nThe `ast.literal_eval()` function, on the other hand, safely parses and evaluates an expression for Python literals (strings, bytes, numbers, tuples, lists, dicts, sets, booleans, and None). It does not evaluate complex expressions, such as function calls, which makes it safer than `eval()`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `eval()` whenever possible, especially with user-supplied input. If you need to evaluate a simple expression, consider using `ast.literal_eval()` instead. It only evaluates Python literals and cannot execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `eval()` function with `ast.literal_eval()`:\n\n```python\nimport ast\n\nvars[\"cIdxVal0\"] = ast.literal_eval(vars[\"cIdxExpr0\"])\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `ast` library, which is a built-in Python library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B307"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-7149f1ef-a5a0-4c77-aacb-dcfd2bdb1cb6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unsafe YAML Load Vulnerability\" in Python is a security vulnerability that occurs when a program uses the `yaml.load()` function from the PyYAML library to parse untrusted input. This function can execute arbitrary Python commands embedded in the YAML input, leading to a potential remote code execution (RCE) vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should never use `yaml.load()` with untrusted input. Instead, use the `yaml.safe_load()` function, which does not execute arbitrary Python commands.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the unsafe `yaml.load()` function with the safe `yaml.safe_load()` function:\n\n```python\ndata = yaml.safe_load(f)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the PyYAML library:\n\n```bash\npip install pyyaml\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B506"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-c92ed013-de1c-4b1e-860c-8ee9cf2ef875",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `eval()` function in Python is a potential security risk as it can execute arbitrary Python code. If an attacker can control the input to `eval()`, they can potentially execute malicious code. This is especially dangerous if your program runs with special privileges.\n\nThe `ast.literal_eval()` function is a safer alternative as it only evaluates Python literals and does not execute arbitrary code. Python literals include strings, bytes, numbers, tuples, lists, dicts, sets, booleans, and `None`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `eval()` with user-supplied input. If you need to evaluate Python literals from a string, use `ast.literal_eval()` instead. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```python\nimport ast\n\nvars[\"cIdxVal1\"] = ast.literal_eval(vars[\"cIdxExpr1\"])\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `ast` library, which is included in the Python Standard Library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B307"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-2fea8236-f93e-43af-9356-f2bc08b4d638",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"File (runScriptName) Vulnerable Due to Permissive Chmod Mask 0o777\" in Python programming language refers to the insecure permission settings for a file. The `os.chmod(runScriptName, 0o777)` command sets the permissions of the file `runScriptName` to `777` (in octal), which means that the file is readable, writable, and executable by all users. This can lead to unauthorized access, modification, or execution of the file, potentially leading to a security breach.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should restrict the permissions of the file to only those necessary for its intended use. For example, if the file only needs to be read and written by the user, but not executed, you can set the permissions to `600` (in octal). If the file needs to be executed by the user, but not by others, you can set the permissions to `700`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the vulnerable line of code:\n\n```python\nos.chmod(runScriptName, 0o777)\n```\n\nwith a more secure line of code, such as:\n\n```python\nos.chmod(runScriptName, 0o600)\n```\n\nor:\n\n```python\nos.chmod(runScriptName, 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` library, which is part of the Python Standard Library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-a65c4467-954b-4478-9779-4dabdeeab60c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `eval()` function in Python is a potential security risk as it can execute arbitrary Python code. If an attacker can control the input to `eval()`, they can run any Python code they wish, including code to harm your system or data. This is known as code injection and is a serious security vulnerability.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `eval()` is used to evaluate a string from a dictionary, which could potentially be manipulated by an attacker:\n\n```python\nvars[\"cIdxVal\"] = eval(vars[\"cIdxExpr\"])\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider using `ast.literal_eval()` instead of `eval()`. The `ast.literal_eval()` function safely parses and evaluates an expression for Python literals (strings, bytes, numbers, tuples, lists, dicts, sets, booleans, and None). It does not evaluate complex expressions or function calls, providing a safer alternative to `eval()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can replace `eval()` with `ast.literal_eval()` in the provided code snippet:\n\n```python\nimport ast\n\nvars[\"cIdxVal\"] = ast.literal_eval(vars[\"cIdxExpr\"])\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `ast` library, which is a built-in Python library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B307"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-562b0fe2-fd73-4d0f-825b-adff39e84f90",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `eval()` function in Python is a potential security risk as it can execute arbitrary Python code. If an attacker can control the input to `eval()`, they can run any Python code they wish, including code to harm your system or data. This is known as code injection and is a serious security vulnerability.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `eval()` is used to evaluate a string from a dictionary, which could potentially be manipulated by an attacker:\n\n```python\nvars[\"cIdxVal\"] = eval(vars[\"cIdxExpr\"])\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider using `ast.literal_eval()` instead of `eval()`. The `ast.literal_eval()` function safely parses and evaluates an expression for Python literals (strings, bytes, numbers, tuples, lists, dicts, sets, booleans, and None). It does not evaluate complex expressions or function calls, providing a safer alternative to `eval()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can replace `eval()` with `ast.literal_eval()` in the provided code snippet:\n\n```python\nimport ast\n\nvars[\"cIdxVal\"] = ast.literal_eval(vars[\"cIdxExpr\"])\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `ast` library, which is a built-in Python library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B307"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-e59a6570-d391-4eab-aec2-a51e9cb04344",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Potential Insecure Function Use - Consider Safer ast.literal_eval\" vulnerability in Python refers to the potential security risks associated with the use of the `eval()` function. The `eval()` function takes a string and evaluates it as a Python expression. This can be dangerous if the string being evaluated is derived from user input or other untrusted sources, as it can lead to arbitrary code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider using `ast.literal_eval()` instead of `eval()`. The `ast.literal_eval()` function safely parses and evaluates an expression node or a string containing a Python literal or container display. Unlike `eval()`, `ast.literal_eval()` does not evaluate complex expressions, only Python literals, making it safer to use with untrusted input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of `eval()` with `ast.literal_eval()`:\n\n```python\nimport ast\n\nvars[\"cidx\"] = ast.literal_eval(vars[\"cIdxExpr\"])\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `ast` library, which is a built-in Python library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B307"
                ]
              }
