            {
              "id": "glog-1bffb4c0-fc11-4984-92bd-bbea82300359",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unsafe YAML Load Vulnerability\" in Python is a security vulnerability that occurs when the `yaml.load()` function is used without specifying a `Loader`. This function can execute arbitrary Python commands, which can lead to remote code execution (RCE) if an attacker can control the input to `yaml.load()`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always use `yaml.safe_load()` instead of `yaml.load()`. The `safe_load()` function can only parse a subset of YAML that excludes all the potentially dangerous constructs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```python\nreturn yaml.safe_load(rawConfig)[\"custom.config\"]\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the PyYAML library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B506"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-5d690f3b-72f5-4ead-a3b9-e69d82de548e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unsafe YAML Load Vulnerability\" in Python is a security flaw that occurs when the `yaml.load()` function is used. This function can create arbitrary Python objects, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code. This is because the `yaml.load()` function does not sanitize input, making it possible for an attacker to inject malicious code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use the `yaml.safe_load()` function instead of `yaml.load()`. The `safe_load()` function only creates simple Python objects like lists or dictionaries.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the vulnerable line of code:\n\n```python\ntensileLibrary = yaml.load(stream, yamlLoader)\n```\n\nwith the safe version:\n\n```python\ntensileLibrary = yaml.safe_load(stream)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the PyYAML library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B506"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-1282cd2d-5521-4bdb-ac90-2bc1c345c597",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"File Permission Vulnerability due to Chmod 0o777 Setting on bytearrayFileName\" in Python programming language refers to the insecure permission setting on a file. The `os.chmod(bytearrayFileName, 0o777)` command sets the permission of the file to 777, which means that all users can read, write, and execute the file. This can lead to unauthorized access, modification, or execution of the file, leading to potential security risks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to set more restrictive permissions on the file. The permissions should be set in a way that only authorized users can access and modify the file. Avoid using 777 permissions unless absolutely necessary.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `os.chmod(bytearrayFileName, 0o777)`, use more restrictive permissions. For example, you can use 644 permissions, which allow the owner to read and write the file, and others to only read the file:\n\n```python\nos.chmod(bytearrayFileName, 0o644)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` library, which is a standard library in Python and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-436d4767-280e-43e6-8dc9-f6ec925aec5f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Potential Insecure Function Use - Consider Safer ast.literal_eval\" refers to the potential security risks associated with the use of Python's `eval()` function. The `eval()` function takes a string and evaluates it as a Python expression. This can be dangerous if the string being evaluated is derived from user input or other untrusted sources, as it can lead to arbitrary code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `eval(valueStr)` is used to evaluate a string as a Python expression. If `valueStr` is derived from an untrusted source, this could lead to a security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider using `ast.literal_eval()` instead of `eval()`. The `ast.literal_eval()` function safely parses and evaluates an expression node or a string containing a Python literal or container display. Unlike `eval()`, `ast.literal_eval()` does not evaluate complex expressions, only literals, making it safer to use with untrusted input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of `eval()` with `ast.literal_eval()`:\n\n```python\nimport ast\n\nreplacedCode[:valStartPos] + str( min(maxVmcnt, ast.literal_eval(valueStr)) ) + \")\"\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `ast` library, which is included in the Python Standard Library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-95: Improper Neutralization of Directives in Dynamically Evaluated Code ('Eval Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/95.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B307"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-936d7313-830e-4372-a6d6-89ec875fe36f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unsafe YAML Load Vulnerability\" in Python is a security vulnerability that occurs when a program uses the `yaml.load()` function from the PyYAML library to parse untrusted input. This function can execute arbitrary Python commands embedded in the YAML input, leading to a potential remote code execution (RCE) vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should never use `yaml.load()` with untrusted input. Instead, use the `yaml.safe_load()` function, which does not execute arbitrary Python commands.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the unsafe `yaml.load()` function with the safe `yaml.safe_load()` function:\n\n```python\ndata = yaml.safe_load(f)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the PyYAML library:\n\n```bash\npip install pyyaml\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B506"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-e8a2105e-2dc0-4e66-9cdc-2307da40f33c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `eval()` function in Python is a potential security risk as it can execute arbitrary Python code. If an attacker can control the input to `eval()`, they can potentially execute malicious code, leading to a variety of attacks such as code injection, denial of service, or even complete system compromise.\n\nThis vulnerability is often referred to as \"Potential Insecurity in Function - Consider Safer `ast.literal_eval` Usage\". The `ast.literal_eval()` function is a safer alternative to `eval()` as it only evaluates Python literals and does not execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `eval()` whenever possible. If you need to evaluate Python literals from a string, use `ast.literal_eval()` instead. Always validate and sanitize input before passing it to any function that executes or evaluates code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace:\n\n```python\nvalue = eval(value)\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```python\nimport ast\nvalue = ast.literal_eval(value)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `ast` module is part of the Python Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B307"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-68c0d879-d0af-4a4a-99bf-68b3430b771d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `eval()` function in Python is a potential security risk as it can execute arbitrary Python code. If an attacker can control the input to `eval()`, they can potentially execute malicious code, leading to a variety of attacks such as code injection, denial of service, or even complete system compromise.\n\nThis vulnerability is often referred to as \"Potential Insecurity in Function - Consider Safer `ast.literal_eval` Usage\". The `ast.literal_eval()` function is a safer alternative to `eval()` as it only evaluates Python literals and does not execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `eval()` whenever possible. If you need to evaluate Python literals from a string, use `ast.literal_eval()` instead. Always validate and sanitize input before passing it to any function that executes or evaluates code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace:\n\n```python\nvalue = eval(value)\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```python\nimport ast\nvalue = ast.literal_eval(value)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `ast` module is part of the Python Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
