            {
              "id": "glog-0c5565ae-669a-41f8-91db-09962e28765b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability arises from the use of the `os.chmod()` function in Python with a mode of `0o777`. This sets the permissions of the file specified by `assemblerFileName` to be readable, writable, and executable by all users. This can lead to unauthorized access, modification, or execution of the file by malicious users or software.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should restrict the permissions of the file to only those necessary for its intended use. For example, if the file only needs to be read and written by the user running the script, you could use a mode of `0o600` instead.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the vulnerable line of code with the following:\n\n```python\nos.chmod(assemblerFileName, 0o600)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` module, which is part of the Python Standard Library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-35c2e926-947d-4072-bd7c-f9ecbc83b27a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `shell=True` subprocess call security vulnerability in Python occurs when a program allows for the execution of shell commands with user-provided input. This can lead to arbitrary command execution if an attacker is able to inject malicious input. This is a serious security vulnerability as it can lead to remote code execution, data breaches, and other security compromises.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `shell=True` in subprocess calls whenever possible. If shell features are required, ensure that user-provided input is properly sanitized and validated before being used in a subprocess call. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `shell=True`, use a list of arguments for the subprocess call. This way, the command and its arguments are passed to the system call directly, without being interpreted by the shell. Here's how you can modify the code:\n\n```python\nfrom subprocess import run, PIPE\n\n# Assuming args is a list of arguments\noutput = run(args, stdout=PIPE).stdout.decode().strip()\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `subprocess` module from Python's standard library. No additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B602"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-edec020f-7f37-43aa-80ae-0e77c0cc76a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unsafe YAML Load Vulnerability\" in Python is a security vulnerability that occurs when a program uses the `yaml.load()` function from the PyYAML library to parse untrusted input. This function can execute arbitrary Python commands embedded in the YAML input, leading to a potential remote code execution (RCE) vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should never use `yaml.load()` with untrusted input. Instead, use the `yaml.safe_load()` function, which does not execute arbitrary Python commands.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the unsafe `yaml.load()` function with the safe `yaml.safe_load()` function:\n\n```python\ndata = yaml.safe_load(stream)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the PyYAML library:\n\n```bash\npip install pyyaml\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
