            {
              "id": "glog-bd64984e-6e42-44db-a26c-26c2dda83cfe",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unsafe YAML Load Vulnerability\" in Python is a security vulnerability that occurs when the `yaml.load()` function is used without specifying a `Loader=` parameter. This function can execute arbitrary Python commands resulting from the processing of a malicious YAML document. This can lead to a variety of attacks, including remote code execution, which can have serious security implications.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use the `safe_load()` function instead of `load()`. The `safe_load()` function only deserializes simple Python objects like integers or lists, and cannot execute arbitrary code.\n\nIf you need to deserialize more complex Python objects and are aware of the security risks, you can use `load()` with the `SafeLoader` parameter.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the vulnerable line of code:\n\n```python\nlogicData = yaml.load(inFile, yamlLoader)\n```\n\nwith the safer alternative:\n\n```python\nlogicData = yaml.safe_load(inFile)\n```\n\nor, if you need to deserialize complex objects:\n\n```python\nlogicData = yaml.load(inFile, Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the PyYAML library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B506"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-4b8ecbb0-4641-42ca-9a38-f91b56d25709",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unsafe YAML Load Vulnerability\" in Python is a security vulnerability that occurs when a program uses the `yaml.load()` function from the PyYAML library to parse untrusted input. This function can execute arbitrary Python commands embedded in the YAML input, leading to a potential remote code execution (RCE) vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should never use `yaml.load()` with untrusted input. Instead, use the `yaml.safe_load()` function, which does not execute arbitrary Python commands.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the unsafe `yaml.load()` function with the safe `yaml.safe_load()` function:\n\n```python\ndata = yaml.safe_load(stream)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the PyYAML library:\n\n```bash\npip install pyyaml\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
