            {
              "id": "glog-69936d32-0dec-42fe-bc60-f2d7625883a3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unsafe YAML Load Vulnerability\" in Python is a security vulnerability that occurs when the `yaml.load()` function is used without specifying a `Loader`. This function can create Python objects and execute arbitrary code, which can lead to Remote Code Execution (RCE) if an attacker is able to control the input to the `yaml.load()` function.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always use `yaml.safe_load()` instead of `yaml.load()`. The `safe_load` function can only parse a subset of YAML that excludes all the potentially dangerous constructs.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the unsafe `yaml.load()` function with the safe `yaml.safe_load()` function:\n\n```python\nimport yaml\n\nwith open('file.yaml', 'r') as f:\n    logicData = yaml.safe_load(f)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the PyYAML library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Python Security - Deserialization](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html#python)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B506"
                ]
              }
            }
